Late accuracy

Actual results from the toy simulation, averaged over the last 60 steps after an abrupt sensor-reliability change. These aren’t real-world performance claims.

The agents had visual, acoustic, or thermal-like binary signals. Midway through each world, visual signals became systematically misleading. Adaptive agents could learn the change from delayed feedback; fixed agents couldn’t.

Here’s the particularly interesting result: greater connectivity amplified both the benefits of adaptation and the damage from outdated assumptions.

Fully connected agents with frozen pre-change beliefs achieved only 44.0% late accuracy, worse than chance. Fully connected adaptive agents achieved 94.2%.

The result is explainable by the known rules of this deliberately simple simulation. It doesn’t prove a novel theory of collective intelligence, but it gives us a working demonstration and a concrete mechanism to investigate.

The key lesson is:

A network doesn’t merely distribute intelligence. It can also distribute error. Whether connectivity helps depends on how its participants update their beliefs.

The discovery hiding in one moment

At simulation step 181, the world state was identical for both policies. They received exactly the same six sensor readings.

The fixed agent confidently made the wrong decision. The adaptive agent made the correct one.

What changed wasn’t the data. It was the meaning assigned to the data.

The adaptive agent had learned that the visual channel was now negatively correlated with reality. Instead of ignoring that channel, it could interpret its readings in reverse.

That’s a useful principle to preserve: a consistently misleading signal can still contain valuable information if its relationship to reality can be learned.

This is precisely why I want Pantheon’s next experiment to be about belief contagion and network structure.

